const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="Nature II (Normativity II) | Elisa Diop-Weyer"
  description="Nature II (Normativity II), by emdw."
>
  <article class="essay-page">
    <a class="back-link" href={`${base}/musings/`}>← Selected Outputs</a>

    <header>
      <h1>Nature II (Normativity II)</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="essay-body">
      <p>Re my digression on the naturalistic fallacy, I think one consequence of rejecting the naturalistic fallacy is that we need not find our meaning in nature. If one finds meaning in something immaterial, “this is immaterial” does not come with a direct normative implication. “This is something that humans have created” does not come with a direct normative implication, and here I am talking about religion and myths. An argument against feats of social engineering cannot be limited to “this does not come from nature”, for the two reasons I outlined, namely (1) there are plenty of consequences of technology that most consider at once unnatural and good and (2)</p>

      <p>it’s not clear to me where one can draw a line to separate the world’s chronology into nature and post nature, or technology in nature and post nature -- so it’s not even clear to me what we’re talking about.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="signature">-- emdw 20261008</p>
  </article>
</Base>

